meta { title = "Modules and exports", description = "Organize source into explicit modules and imports.", order = 10, group = "Language reference" }

Each .k file is a module. Declarations are private unless exported. Imports name exactly which declarations enter the current scope, with optional aliases. Relative imports include their .k extension.

h2 { "Export a function" }

In math.k:

codeblock { language = kalandra, """export fn double(value: int) -> int:
  value * 2
""" }

In main.k in the same directory:

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "./math.k" import double as twice

fn main() -> int:
  twice(21)
""" }

codeblock { language = bash, """kalandra-check main.k
kalandra main.k -o program
./program
""" }

h2 { "Export types deliberately" }

Record, enum and error declarations can be exported and imported. Aliasing preserves their nominal identity rather than creating a new type. A helper continues resolving its own private names in its declaring module; importing it does not expose all of that module.

h2 { "Re-export a public surface" }

An explicit re-export lets a library entry expose selected functions or types from internal modules. Re-exports preserve the original declaration identity and helper scope. Use a small public entry instead of requiring callers to know the internal directory layout.

h2 { "Use a package name" }

A bare import such as from "geometry" import total refers to an explicit package mapping or installed dependency. The compiler does not guess a module from your working directory or fetch a missing library while building.

link { url = "/docs/projects", "Configure project entry and local package mappings" }

